 "result_text": "B902 no longer flags metaclasses that follow the usual conventions. A class counts as a metaclass when `type` is among its bases.\n\n- **Instance methods** (such as `__init__`) in a metaclass may use `cls` or `self` as the first argument.\n- **`__new__` and classmethods** in a metaclass may use `metacls`, `metaclass`, `typ` or `mcs`, as well as the usual `cls`.\n\nOn a quick test file with a metaclass using `cls` in `__init__` and `mcs` in `__new__`, nothing was flagged. A method whose first argument was `this` was still flagged.\n\nThe test suite gives 14 passed and 1 failed. The failure is `test_selfclean_bugbear`, which fails the same way without my change, from a W504 line-break warning on line 177 and on one other line (322 in the current file, 320 before my edit). I didn't touch that, and I added no tests.\n\nThe change is in `bugbear.py`, in `check_for_b902` and the `B902` constants.",
